{
  "image_name": "a1e4808ac26bef4c339721a4adfa66e2.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two views of how species’ geographic ranges shift under climate warming across a landscape of latitude and elevation. In the simpler, temperature-only view, warming moves the suitable range uphill and poleward: populations newly appear at the cool leading edge, persist in the middle, and disappear at the warm trailing edge once temperatures exceed a threshold. The dashed and solid outlines indicate an earlier versus later range, showing simultaneous expansion into newly suitable habitat and contraction where conditions become too warm.\n\nThe lower half shows that real range shifts often depart from this idealized temperature-driven pattern because many other factors intervene. Habitat fragmentation and land use can block movement, dispersal barriers can leave suitable areas uncolonized, and interactions with competitors can prevent establishment even where climate seems favorable. Other labels indicate that rapid evolution or acclimation may allow persistence beyond expected limits, while climate extremes, local buffering, and thermal niche differences can alter where edges actually move. The figure’s overall message is that warming often pushes ranges, but observed shifts reflect a combination of climate, landscape, ecology, and species-specific responses.",
